WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.067
-Our next guest represents

00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:03.601
New York's
14th Congressional District

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:05.868
in the House of Representatives.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:08.534
Congresswoman
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:10.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:17.434
♪♪

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:24.501
♪♪

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:27.067
Well, it is so lovely
to have you here.

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:28.367
-Lovely to be here.
Thank you.

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:30.100
-And it seems like
we planned this,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.467
but this has been scheduled
for a long time.

00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:35.434
No one could have imagined
a couple of months ago

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.667
what happened today.
How does this feel?

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:40.000
How are you processing it
as a New Yorker?

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.534
-Yeah, well, I mean,

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:44.033
there's as an American
and as a New Yorker.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:49.200
As a New Yorker, it was
a very New York kind of day.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.567
You have not just
what was happening

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.267
with former President Trump,

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:56.400
but then you have MTG
flying into town.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.133
You've got, you know, just
the whole chaos of the scene

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:02.367
and also all the people
walking by like,

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:04.501
"Okay. I'm on my lunch break.
It's time to go."

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:07.000
-Yeah.
-It's -- You know --

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.968
It was -- It was just a lot.
It was a circus, right?

00:01:09.968 --> 00:01:12.400
-I mean, your whole life,
this is a person who,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.267
especially in New York,
has been investigated,

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:18.133
never charged.

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:21.367
Multiple --
certainly accusations

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:23.400
of financial improprieties.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.067
Did you ever think
this day would come

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:26.501
where he would be arraigned,

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:29.834
especially for things like,
you know, the misuse of money?

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:31.834
-Yeah, I mean, I --

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:36.934
I think I had always felt
that something might happen.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:38.767
Honestly,
I think if something did,

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.868
I would have thought it would be
something like this,

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:45.200
around falsification of business
documents or some sort of charge

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.701
that has to do with
documentation or finances.

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:50.133
But, I mean,
my dad was an architect.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:53.634
He worked in construction
and in that industry.

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.234
And from the time
I was a kid,

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:59.067
it's like no one knew this guy
better than New Yorkers.

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:02.934
And just, like,
this is just --

00:02:02.934 --> 00:02:04.100
People would have thought

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.667
this is what
he would have been in for

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.434
before he was president.
-Yeah.

00:02:07.434 --> 00:02:11.400
-So, you know, I think it does
feel like a matter of course.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.601
I also think that --
I mean, listen.

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:17.167
This is not about guaranteeing
one outcome or another.

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:22.567
This is about due process,
and the lack of process of,

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:25.901
I think, a formal due process
for so long

00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:27.634
really created this question of,

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:30.734
do we really have
an equal society,

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:33.100
no matter your income,
no matter how powerful you are?

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.667
And I think the presence
of this process demonstrates

00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:39.667
that we should have one law
that applies to all people,

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:42.000
no matter who you are
or where you come from.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:47.033
-A very true statement.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.901
And also, look.
This is the process.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.567
He's innocent
until proven guilty.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.434
And you were talking
a little backstage

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:54.801
about how there is oftentimes
a hesitation,

00:02:54.801 --> 00:02:57.834
both in legal circles
and in politics,

00:02:57.834 --> 00:03:02.534
not to move forward unless
you're certain of the outcome.

00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:04.334
-Mm-hmm. Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:06.801
And I think -- I do. I see this
in a legislative context.

00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:08.968
Sometimes you see this
in a legal context,

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:11.100
which is we don't want to try

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.133
unless we know what the outcome
is before we start, right?

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:15.801
We don't want to hold
a vote on something

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:18.767
unless we know for sure
that it's going to pass.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:21.000
We know that --
You know, the question.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.467
There's been so much legal
speculation over the last week.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:25.868
Is this a good idea?
Is it not a good idea?

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:29.501
If we -- If there is evidence
for a crime,

00:03:29.501 --> 00:03:32.968
which is what
Alvin Bragg has asserted,

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:34.300
and put before a grand jury

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.834
and before a jury
of one's peers,

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:40.334
they themselves move forward
with an indictment,

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:44.200
then this is the process
that must happen in our society.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.501
And then, once again,
there will be another jury

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:48.501
of their peers
to convict or acquit.

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:50.167
But we should not make
these decisions

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:53.534
solely on whether you know
that you're guaranteed

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:54.968
to be successful from the start.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.501
I think people need
to see these charges

00:03:57.501 --> 00:04:00.667
or see just charges
where there is evidence for it.

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:04.701
And I believe that, you know,
the presence of that due process

00:04:04.701 --> 00:04:07.434
is what makes our system fair.

00:04:07.434 --> 00:04:09.701
It's a consequence
in and of itself.

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:12.834
And I think it's important.
I just think it's important.

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:14.501
-You mentioned Alvin Bragg.

00:04:14.501 --> 00:04:18.434
Three GOP House chairs
basically wrote to him

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:20.267
demanding that he come
and testify,

00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:24.200
that he bring documents
from this investigation.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.400
It seems like the office hit
back pretty strongly to that.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.033
How do you feel about sort of
the members of Congress

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:32.234
reaching out and trying
to interfere

00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:34.901
in an investigation like this?
-Yeah, I mean,

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:37.167
I think what they've attempted
to do,

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:40.100
whether it's Jim Jordan

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.133
or Chairman Comer
in House Oversight,

00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:45.734
where I sit, there needs
to be clear jurisdiction.

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:49.367
And what we have seen from
House Republicans especially

00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:52.367
is that they seem to have
interpreted their role

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:56.167
as just defending and working
for Donald Trump,

00:04:56.167 --> 00:05:00.167
sometimes more than working
for the kinds of people

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:02.234
that they've been
elected to serve.

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:05.467
You know, we're seeing
more energy for defending

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:09.334
former President Trump than for
passing gun-safety legislation,

00:05:09.334 --> 00:05:13.667
from helping health care,
from people in, you know --

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:16.834
from what happened
in the spill in, you know --

00:05:16.834 --> 00:05:18.467
I think what we need to see

00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:21.367
is an actual government
that works for people,

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:23.534
no matter what party
you're a part of,

00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:26.534
instead of which partisan person
you're most loyal to.

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:29.701
-I think that's --
They should also know...

00:05:29.701 --> 00:05:31.634
[ Applause ]

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:34.667
When you work for Donald Trump,
you often don't get paid.

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.667
And that's something
they should keep in mind.

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:39.968
You questioned Michael Cohen.
This was a few years back.

00:05:39.968 --> 00:05:42.868
This was your first year. You
were a freshman congresswoman.

00:05:42.868 --> 00:05:46.133
And you talked to him
about the inflation of assets.

00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:48.334
This was a different thing
that Donald Trump did for years.

00:05:48.334 --> 00:05:51.000
He would -- Basically, if he was
counting it as an asset,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.801
he would say
it was worth this much.

00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:55.033
And if he was paying taxes
on it, he would maybe lower it.

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.133
And this actually led --
Tish James has mentioned you

00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:58.701
and mentioned that questioning
as leading

00:05:58.701 --> 00:06:03.133
to a separate civil case
against Donald Trump.

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:05.033
It's so interesting because
I feel like a lot of times

00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:06.434
you watch people
grandstand in moments

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:08.300
where they get to
question someone.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.100
You laid out -- very simple,
in a way

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.234
that people like even I
could understand.

00:06:13.234 --> 00:06:15.133
Do you feel like
people in Congress

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.234
miss the opportunity
to take their time

00:06:17.234 --> 00:06:18.934
and lay something out
very clearly

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:20.767
for the American people
to understand?

00:06:20.767 --> 00:06:23.267
-Yeah, you know, I, um --
I actually recently --

00:06:23.267 --> 00:06:25.367
So, my first year,

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:28.434
I also held this
kind of Twitter seminar

00:06:28.434 --> 00:06:30.067
for some of my colleagues.

00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:33.501
And this year, I worked with
some of my other colleagues,

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:38.734
including folks like
Maxine Waters and Madeleine Dean

00:06:38.734 --> 00:06:40.868
on also kind of teaching folks

00:06:40.868 --> 00:06:43.968
how to use your five minutes
most effectively.

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:45.801
I think, you know --
When we talk about

00:06:45.801 --> 00:06:47.534
what your member
of Congress does,

00:06:47.534 --> 00:06:49.100
it's not just legislate.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.834
Our job is to investigate.

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:54.868
And that is a core element
of our duties, to pursue that.

00:06:54.868 --> 00:06:57.934
And so I do think --
It takes a lot of preparation.

00:06:57.934 --> 00:06:59.667
It's kind of like
what Mark Twain said.

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:03.667
"This letter would be shorter,
but I didn't have time."

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:06.400
That brevity takes an enormous
amount of preparation.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.367
I kind of look like that scene

00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:09.901
from "It's Always Sunny
in Philadelphia"

00:07:09.901 --> 00:07:11.167
with the guy like this.

00:07:11.167 --> 00:07:13.033
-All the yarn connecting...
-All the yarn.

00:07:13.033 --> 00:07:15.901
That's what my desk looks like
during a hearing sometimes.

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:19.567
You have to work with
what's happening midstream.

00:07:19.567 --> 00:07:22.334
But I think it's very powerful.
It's really important.

00:07:22.334 --> 00:07:25.801
And our job is to educate
and communicate to the public,

00:07:25.801 --> 00:07:27.467
in addition to legislating.

00:07:27.467 --> 00:07:30.834
-I want to talk about other
legislation you're working on.

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:32.968
Before we go to commercial,

00:07:32.968 --> 00:07:35.901
Trump was supposedly stuck on
FDR after the arraignment today.

00:07:35.901 --> 00:07:38.501
Is that the most perfect
New York punishment for...?

00:07:38.501 --> 00:07:41.534
-Okay.
I have to take issue

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:44.133
because I represent
LaGuardia Airport.

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:46.100
-I told you. It's nice now.
-Okay?

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.601
-I told you. It's nice now.

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:49.300
-We put a lot of work into that.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.033
-And I'm telling you.
It is great.

00:07:51.033 --> 00:07:52.901
I'm a huge fan of LaGuardia now.

00:07:52.901 --> 00:07:56.067
It's just hard for us as comedy
writers. You took someth--

00:07:56.067 --> 00:07:57.901
This would be like if
the Mets won the World Series.

00:07:57.901 --> 00:07:59.767
-I know.
-What would we do?

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:01.167
-I know.
What would we joke about?

00:08:01.167 --> 00:08:03.968
What I will say is
that I do think it was an omen

00:08:03.968 --> 00:08:06.501
that he landed
in my congressional district.

00:08:06.501 --> 00:08:08.734
-[ Laughs ] Yeah. I think --
-Show him who's boss!

00:08:08.734 --> 00:08:10.300
-There you go.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.501
We'll be right back
with more from Congresswoman

00:08:12.501 --> 00:08:14.300
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

